    {
      "sample_index": 2,
      "text": "1. **Understand the Composition of Functions:**\n\n   We are given a composition of functions \\( f = q \\circ p \\circ r \\), which means \\( f(x) = q(p(r(x))) \\).\n\n2. **Invertibility and Trace:**\n\n   Since \\( p(x), q(x) \\), and \\( r(x) \\) are all invertible, their inverses exist and are denoted as \\( p^{-1}(x), q^{-1}(x) \\), and \\( r^{-1}(x) \\) respectively.\n\n3. **Applying the Invertible Properties:**\n\n   We need to find the inverse \\( f^{-1}(x) \\) of the composition \\( f(x) = q(p(r(x))) \\).\n\n4. **Two-Inverse Property for Invertible Functions:**\n\n   We apply the property for inverting composite functions: if \\( f(x) = g(h(x)) \\), then \\( f^{-1}(x) \\) can be expressed as \\( h^{-1}(g^{-1}(x)) \\).\n\n5. **Apply to Our Functions:**\n\n   Here, \\( g = q \\) and \\( h = p \\) in the form \\( p(r(x)) \\), so we have:\n   \\[\n   f^{-1}(x) = r^{-1}(q^{-1}(x))\n   \\]\n\n6. **Identify the Correct Expression:**\n\n   The expression given in option C matches our derived relation:\n   \\[\n   r^{-1}(q^{-1}(x)) = r^{-1} \\circ q^{-1} \\circ p^{-1}\n   \\]\n\n### Conclusion:\n\\[\n\\boxed{C}\n\\]",
